      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0051",
      "sequence": 63,
      "meaning": "Satī wonders: Viṣṇu, who assumes a human body for the gods’ welfare, is omniscient like Śiva; could that abode of knowledge and enemy of demons wander seeking a woman like an ignorant man? Yet Śiva’s words cannot be false, for everyone knows him to be all-knowing. Her immense doubt does not yield to insight. Though she says nothing, the indwelling Śiva knows all and cautions her not to harbor such suspicion merely from a woman’s disposition: this Raghubīra is his chosen deity, whose story Agastya sang and whose devotion Śiva taught the sage—the Lord whom steadfast sages always serve.",
