    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0053",
      "sequence": 65,
      "meaning": "Lakṣmaṇa sees the guise fashioned by Umā and is astonished, but, grave and wise, says nothing because he knows the Lord’s power. Rāma, Lord of the gods, sees through Satī’s deception; the omniscient knower of every heart is the very one whose remembrance dispels ignorance. Satī still tries to conceal herself—such is the force of a woman’s nature. Mindful of his own māyā’s power, Rāma smiles and speaks gently. Joining his palms, he bows, identifies himself with his father’s name, and asks why Vṛṣaketu is absent and why she wanders alone in the forest. Hearing Rāma’s gentle but profound words, Satī is deeply embarrassed and returns fearfully toward Maheśa with great anxiety in her heart.",
